-- title:
--   Recovering all structural conclusions on the good event
-- statement:
--   Let $\Theta$ be a nonempty compact metric space with its Borel measurable structure and let $\theta:\Theta\to\mathbb R^d$ be continuous. Write $f_\mu(x)=\int\varphi_d(x-\theta(a))\,d\mu(a)$ and $L_w(\mu)=\sum_iw_i\log f_\mu(x_i)$ for probability laws $\mu$ on $\Theta$. Write $\Delta(\mu,\mu_0)=L_1(\mu_0)-L_1(\mu)$ and $J(\mu,\mu_0)=\sum_i[\log(f_\mu(x_i)/f_{\mu_0}(x_i))]^2$. The structural failure set $F(R,t,a)$ consists of data–weight pairs for which some coordinate satisfies $w_i\le0$ or $|w_i-1|>a$, the weighted optimizer is nonunique, some optimizer lacks finite support of cardinality at most $R$, or some weighted/ordinary optimizer pair has $\Delta>t$ or $J>t$. Assume $n\ge1$ and $(x,w)\notin F(R,t,a)$, with $R,t,a\in\mathbb R$. Then the weights are positive, $\max_i|w_i-1|\le a$, and there is a unique weighted optimizer $\mu$ with finite support of cardinality at most $R$. Against every ordinary optimizer $\mu_0$, $$0\le\Delta(\mu,\mu_0)\le t,\qquad J(\mu,\mu_0)\le t.$$ This converts absence of failures into an existence-and-uniqueness statement over all probability measures.
